y 1..x = sum of popcount(i) for i=1..x. Let's call countBits(x). Then the remaining N - countBits(x) elements come from integer x+1's powerful array (the smallest exponents first since sorted ascending).\n\nSo:\n1. Binary search x such that countBits(x) <= N < countBits(x+1). Actually find largest x with countBits(x) <= N.\n2. Sum of exponents for integers 1..x: call sumBits(x) = sum over i=1..x of sum of exponents in i.\n3. remaining = N - countBits(x). Take the `remaining` smallest set bits of (x+1). Their exponents sum.\n\nNeed efficient countBits and sumBits.\n\ncountBits(x) = number of set bits in all numbers from 1 to x. Standard: for each bit position b, count of numbers in [1,x] with bit b set.\n\nCount of numbers in [0,x] with bit b set: \nfull cycles = (x+1) // (2^(b+1)) → each contributes 2^b ones.\nremainder = (x+1) % (2^(b+1)); extra = max(0, remainder - 2^b).\ntotal = full*2^b + extra.\n\nsumBits(x) = sum over bit b of b * (count of numbers in [1,x] with bit b set) = sum over b of b * countBitB(x).\n\nBoth computed by iterating bits up to ~50 (since x up to 10^15, and N up to 10^15, x could be up to... N elements, each integer contributes at least 1, so x <= N <= 10^15, ~50 bits).\n\nBinary search x in [0, upper]. Upper bound: since each integer >=1 element, x <= N. Use N as upper.\n\nLet me code countBits and sumBits.\n\ndef bitCount(x): # numbers in [0,x] set bits total... but we want [1,x], same as [0,x] since 0 contributes nothing.\n\nLet me define count(x) = total set bits in [0,x]. For x < 0 return 0.\n\nFor b in range(bits):\n    p = 1<<(b+1)\n    total += (x+1)//p * (1<<b)\n    r = (x+1)%p\n    total += max(0, r - (1<<b))\n\nsumExp(x) = sum over b of b * (count of set bit b in [0,x]).\n\nThen prefixSum(N):\n if N==0: return 0\n find largest x with count(x) <= N. Binary search.\n base = sumExp(x)\n remaining = N - count(x)\n # take remaining smallest bits of x+1\n next_num = x+1\n # iterate exponents ascending\n cnt=0; b=0; extra=0\n temp = next