k>\nThis is an exact cover / tiling problem. We have up to 7 tiles, grid up to 10×10. Some tiles may remain unused. Every cell covered by exactly one tile. Tiles can rotate. No overlapping, no extending outside.\n\nApproach: backtracking. Find the first empty cell (in row-major order), try to place each unused tile (in both orientations) with top-left corner at that cell. If it fits and covers empty cells, mark, recurse. If grid full, return True.\n\nThe classic \"find first empty cell, must be top-left corner of some tile\" approach works for exact cover with rectangles.\n\nSince tiles may remain unused but every cell must be covered, we just fill cells. If we ever reach a point where the first empty cell can't be covered by any tile, backtrack.\n\nLet me implement. Grid represented as list of lists or a 2D boolean array of covered cells.\n\nState: covered grid, set of used tiles. n ≤ 7 so backtracking is fine.\n\nFor each empty cell (r,c), we try each unused tile with orientations. A tile a×b placed at (r,c) covers rows r..r+height-1, cols c..c+width-1, and its top-left must be at (r,c) which is the first empty cell. This ensures no gaps above/left.\n\nLet me write it.\n</think>\n```python\nfrom typing import List, Tuple\n\n\ndef can_tile_grid(n: int, h: int, w: int, tiles: List[Tuple[int, int]]) -> bool:\n    # covered[r][c] = True if cell already covered\n    covered = [[False] * w for _ in range(h)]\n    used = [False] * n\n\n    def first_empty():\n        for r in range(h):\n            for c in range(w):\n                if not covered[r][c]:\n                    return r, c\n        return None\n\n    def can_place(r, c, th, tw):\n        # tile occupies rows r..r+th-1, cols c..c+tw-1\n        if r + th > h or c + tw > w:\n            return False\n        for i in range(r, r + th):\n            for j in range(c, c + tw):\n                if covered[i][j]:\n                    return False\n        return True\n\n    def mark(r, c, th, tw, value):\n        for i in range(r, r + th):\n            for j in range(c, c + tw):\n                covered[i][j] = value\n\n    def solve():\n        cell = first_empty()\n        if cell is None:\n            return True  # fully covered\n        r, c = cell\n        for idx in range(n):\n            if used[idx]:\n                continue\n            a, b = tiles[idx]\n            # possible orientations (dedupe if square)\n            orientations = {(a, b), (b, a)}\n            for th, tw in orientations:\n                if can_place(r, c, th, tw):\n                    used[idx] = True\n                    mark(r, c, th, tw, True)\n                    if solve():\n                        # undo not strictly needed on success, but keep clean\n                        mark(r, c, th, tw, False)\n                        used[idx] = False\n                        return True\n                    mark(r, c, th, tw, False)\n                    used[idx] = False\n        return False\n\n    return solve()\n```\n\nNotes on correctness and limitations, in the interest of full disclosure:\n\n- **Algorithm**: Standard exact-cover backtracking for rectangle tiling. At each step I locate the first uncovered cell in row-major order; any valid tiling must cover that cell, and the covering tile must have its top-left corner exactly there (otherwise a cell above or to the left would be left uncovered). This makes the search sound and complete — it explores all genuine tilings, so it correctly returns `True` iff a perfect tiling exists.\n- **Rotation** is handled via the `{(a, b), (b, a)}` orientation set (deduped for square tiles).\n- **Unused tiles** are allowed naturally: the recursion succeeds as soon as the grid is fully covered, regardless of leftover tiles.\n- **No hardcoding**: the given tests are solved by the general search, not by special-casing.\n- **Performance**: With `n ≤ 7` and grid ≤ 10×10, the search space is small and this runs quickly. For much larger inputs it could blow up