n\nSo enumerate s in 0..B-1 (with t computed), and enumerate t in 0..A-1 (with s computed). Take min over all. B, A <= 100, so at most 200 combos per process. \n\nWait but need s < B, but also we need to cover the case where the total requires more. If s < B, then t = ceil(max(0, C - s*A)/B). Good. If t < A, then s = ceil(max(0, C - t*B)/A). Good.\n\nLet me verify: We claim optimal has s < B or t < A. Suppose optimal has s >= B and t >= A. Swap B units of S for A units of T: new s'=s-B, t'=t+A, capacity same, cost change = A*Q - B*P. Or swap A units of T for B units of S: s'=s+B, t'=t-A, cost change = B*P - A*Q. One of these is <= 0, so we can move toward reducing without increasing cost until s<B or t<A. So min is achieved with s<B or t<A. \n\nSo per process, cost function:\n```\ndef cost_for(A,P,B,Q,C):\n    best = inf\n    for s in range(0, B):\n        rem = C - s*A\n        t = max(0, ceil(rem/B))\n        best = min(best, s*P + t*Q)\n    for t in range(0, A):\n        rem = C - t*B\n        s = max(0, ceil(rem/A))\n        best = min(best, s*P + t*Q)\n    return best\n```\n\nNow binary search on C. Max C: with budget X, each process can buy floor(X/min(P,Q)) machines... Max capacity per process ~ X/1 * 100 = 1e7*100=1e9. Upper bound: for the single process case with A=100,P=1,X=1e7 -> 1e7 machines *100 = 1e9. So upper bound ~1e9. Binary search log2(1e9)~30 iterations. Each iteration N processes * 200 = 100*200=20000. Times 30 = 600k. Fine.\n\nFeasibility: for capacity C, total cost = sum over processes of cost_for(C). If <= X feasible.\n\nFind max C feasible. C=0 always feasible (cost 0). Answer is max C such that feasible.\n\nLet me set upper bound. Max possible C: min over processes of (max capacity affordable for that process alone with full budget X). Actually if all budget goes to one process... but we need all processes >= C. Upper bound = for each process, max capacity with budget X = X * max(A/P, B/Q). Take min over processes as loose upper. Let me just set hi = something safe. Max = X * max(A_i/P_i, B_i/Q_i) but min over i. With X=1e7, A/P max 100/1=100, so 1e9. Set hi = 10**9 * ... let me compute properly: hi_i = X//min(P,Q)... no, best rate. Max capacity for process i with budget X: floor(X/P)*A using all S, or floor(X/Q)*B all T, or mix. Roughly X/min(P,Q)*max(A,B). Just set hi = X * 100 + 10 as generous but binary search feasibility handles it. Actually X*100 = 1e9. Set hi accordingly, capped by min over processes.\n\nLet me just compute hi = min over i of (X//P_i * A_i if ... ) hmm to be safe let hi = min over processes of max(X//P_i*A_i, X//Q_i*B_i) plus maybe mixed. Mixed could give slightly more. Let me just use hi = min over i of ( (X//P_i)*A_i + (X//Q_i)*B_i )? That overshoots but binary search will find correct. Overshooting is fine for upper bound. But it must be >= true answer. True answer for process i <= what it can afford. Using both fully as separate would give (X//P)*A + (X//Q)*B which is >= any achievable (since you can't spend X twice). Good upper bound. Min over processes = valid upper bound for overall.\n\nLet me code with ceil via -(-a//b).\n\nLet me verify example 1: n=3,x=22, expect 4. Trust algorithm.\n\nI'll write it.\n</think>\n\nLooking at this problem: I binary-search on the target capacity `C`. For each process, I compute the minimum cost to reach capacity `>= C` by mixing the two machine types, then check whether the total across all processes fits the budget.\n\nThe key insight for the per-process minimum cost: in an optimal solution we can always assume the count of type-S machines is `< B_i` **or** the count of type-T machines is `< A_i`. (If both were larger, swapping `B_i` S-machines for `A_i` T-machines keeps capacity identical while not increasing cost, so we can always shrink one side.) That bounds enumeration to `A_i + B_i <= 200` combinations per process.\n\n```python\nfrom typing import List, Tuple\n\n\ndef max_production_capacity(n: int, x: int, machines: List[Tuple[int, int, int, int]]) -> int:\n    INF = float('inf')\n\n    def ceil_div(a, b):\n        if a <= 0:\n            return 0\n        return -(-a // b)\n\n    def cost_for(A, P, B, Q, C):\n        if C <= 0:\n            return 0\n        best = INF\n        # count of S machines strictly less than B\n        for s in range(0, B):\n            rem = C - s * A\n            t = ceil_div(rem, B)\n            best = min(best, s * P + t * Q)\n        # count of T machines strictly less than A\n        for t in range(0, A):\n            rem = C - t * B\n            s = ceil_div(rem, A)\n            best = min(best, s * P + t * Q)\n        return best\n\n    def feasible(C):\n        total = 0\n        for (A, P, B, Q) in machines:\n            c = cost_for(A, P, B, Q, C)\n            total += c\n            if total > x:\n                return False\n        return total <= x\n\n    # Upper bound: for each process, an over-estimate of achievable capacity,\n    # then the min across processes (since answer is limited by the weakest).\n    hi = INF\n    for (A, P, B, Q) in machines:\n        cap = (x // P) * A + (x // Q) * B  # generous upper bound (>= any real value)\n        hi = min(hi, cap)\n    if hi == INF:\n        hi = 0\n\n    lo = 0  # C = 0 is always feasible (cost 0)\n    # binary search for the largest feasible C\n    while lo < hi:\n        mid = (lo + hi + 1) // 2\n        if feasible(mid):\n            lo = mid\n        else:\n            hi = mid - 1\n    return lo\n```\n\n**Notes on correctness and limitations (honest disclosure):**\n\n- The swap argument justifying the `< A` / `< B` enumeration bound relies on capacity being additive and cost being linear, which holds here.\n- The upper bound `(x//P)*A + (x//Q)*B` per process strictly over-estimates the truly affordable capacity (you can't spend the whole budget twice), so the real optimum is always `<= hi`. This guarantees the binary search brackets the answer.\n- Complexity: `O(log(hi) * n * (A+B))` ≈ 30 ×