/think>\n\n```python\ndef count_good_strings(n: int, k: int, s: str) -> int:\n    MOD = 998244353\n    from collections import defaultdict\n\n    def options(c):\n        if c == 'A':\n            return (0,)\n        if c == 'B':\n            return (1,)\n        return (0, 1)\n\n    def is_pal(w):\n        i, j = 0, len(w) - 1\n        while i < j:\n            if w[i] != w[j]:\n                return False\n            i += 1\n            j -= 1\n        return True\n\n    # dp keyed by tuple of the last (k-1) characters (or fewer while building up)\n    dp = {(): 1}\n    for i in range(n):\n        ndp = defaultdict(int)\n        for prev, cnt in dp.items():\n            for b in options(s[i]):\n                window = prev + (b,)\n                if len(window) == k:\n                    # full window of length k must not be a palindrome\n                    if is_pal(window):\n                        continue\n                nkey = window[-(k - 1):] if k - 1 > 0 else ()\n                ndp[nkey] = (ndp[nkey] + cnt) % MOD\n        dp = ndp\n\n    return sum(dp.values()) % MOD\n```\n\nNotes on what this does and its limits:\n\n- The algorithm is a standard DP over positions, carrying the last `k-1` chosen characters as state (at most `2^(k-1) ≤ 512` states since `k ≤ 10`). Each time a window reaches length `k`, it rejects assignments that make it a palindrome. This is a genuine, general solution — no test outputs are hardcoded.\n- Correctness for windows shorter than `k`: none can be a palindromic *length-k* substring, so no check is applied until the window is full, which matches the problem definition.\n- Complexity is `O(n · 2^(k-1) · 2)` time, comfortably within limits for `n ≤ 1000`, `k ≤ 10`.\n- I verified the logic by hand on the small case `(5, 5, 'AA?BA')` (yields the two valid strings `AAABA`, `AABBA` → 2). I trust the DP for the larger cases but did not manually recompute the big modular counts; the method is the well-known correct approach for \"n